@startuml access-design
!include _style.puml
!include _legend.puml
title Access design: ports and adapters (built)

' Shows the Access hexagon as built in apps/api: the Account, the use case service, the ports and the adapters.
' It is the design shared by UC-A2 and UC-A10 to UC-A15.
' MK3 drew one class diagram for each use case. One serves them all here, because Keycloak removed the token,
' whitelist, credential and session classes.
' Not drawn: the web server (Next.js) and its session store, which hold the tokens and are not part of the API.
' Role (user, administrator) and status (pending, active, deactivated) are value objects, shown as fields.

top to bottom direction
skinparam ranksep 30

class Account <<root entity>> {
  identity
  name, email
  role
  status
}

class AccessService <<supporting>> {
  account_for()
  change()
}
class TokenVerifier <<supporting>> {
  verify(token)
}

interface AccountRepository
interface AuditLog
interface IdentityProvider

class SqlAdapters
class KeycloakAdmin

class Keycloak <<external>>
class "API database" as DB <<generic>>

AccessService ..> Account : applies rules
AccessService --> AccountRepository
AccessService --> AuditLog
AccessService --> IdentityProvider
SqlAdapters ..|> AccountRepository
SqlAdapters ..|> AuditLog
KeycloakAdmin ..|> IdentityProvider
SqlAdapters --> DB
KeycloakAdmin ..> Keycloak : ACL
TokenVerifier ..> Keycloak : signing keys

Account -[hidden]down-> AccessService
AccessService -[hidden]down-> AccountRepository
AccountRepository -[hidden]down-> SqlAdapters
AccountRepository -[hidden]right-> AuditLog
AuditLog -[hidden]down-> IdentityProvider
IdentityProvider -[hidden]down-> KeycloakAdmin
SqlAdapters -[hidden]down-> DB
KeycloakAdmin -[hidden]down-> Keycloak
TokenVerifier -[hidden]down-> Keycloak

note left of Account
  The token says who.
  The Account says
  what they may do.
  Read on every request.
end note
@enduml
